      "annotations": [
        "R1 acts as an agent for the downstream call, so it needs a person token for R2 before it can get a resource token there.",
        "It routes this to the person server named by the ps claim of the upstream auth token — NOT the ps claim in its own agent token, which names R1's person server and not the person's.",
        "upstream_token is a body parameter. R1 still signs with its own key and presents its own agent token via Signature-Key; the upstream token is proof of the authorization being extended, not a signing credential.",
        "The PS verifies the upstream token per upstream token verification: its aud must equal the iss of R1's agent token, which is what proves the upstream token was issued to the party now making this request.",
        "The PS mints a downstream-directed sub for R2 and binds cnf to R1's key. The upstream sub is never carried forward: it is meaningless under a new issuer and would be exactly the correlation handle pairwise identifiers exist to prevent."
      ],
      "is_response": false
    },
